\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Notation}
\title{Notation for arithmetic families}
\date{2026-11-13}

\p{The algebraic closure is #{\cl{\mathbb Q}}
(\ref{fdip-0049}, \ref{fdip-00QZ}).}

\p{The prime spectrum is #{\Spec}. The basic open of an element is
#{\basic{g}} (\ref{fdip-000U}, \ref{fdip-00R1}).}

\p{The additive character of #{\mathbb F_p} is #{\zeta_p^{t}}
(\ref{fdip-008O}, \ref{fdip-00QY}).}

\p{Arithmetic brings closures, prime spectra and basic open sets, and
they take the names algebra gives them, written so that none collides
with an earlier symbol. The closure has its own mark because the bar
is conjugation; #{\Spec} of a ring is distinct from #{\spec} of an
operator; a basic open set is #{\basic{g}} because #{D} names
operators. A character of #{\mathbb F_p} is the root of unity
#{\zeta_p^{t}}, so that #{e} keeps its Part I meaning.}
